                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/infinity-limits+-+contents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#distributive_categories_and_cartesian_closure"} Distributive categories and cartesian closure
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong sind-object
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            is a
            %strong
              formal
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sifted+colimit"} sifted colimit
            of objects of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+presheaves"} category of presheaves
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+cocompletion"} free cocompletion
            of
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            is written
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi sind
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sind
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SInd
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SInd(\mathcal{C})
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          %p
            There is an analogy here with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ind-objects"} ind-objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ind
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ind(\mathcal{C})
            is the filtered colimit completion of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          %p
            Typically the main case of interest here is the situation where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            is small with finite coproducts. If a small category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            has finite coproducts, the sifted colimit completion comprises the finite-product-preserving presheaves:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sind
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi FP
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sind(\mathcal{C})\cong FP(\mathcal{C}^{op},Set)
          %p
            The analogy here is that if a small category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            has finite
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ind
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi Lex
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ind(\mathcal{C})\cong Lex(\mathcal{C}^{op},Set)
          %p
            One reason this is often relevant because if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}^\op
            has finite
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic theory
          %p
            Another point of relevance is that this is a canonical way of building a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed category
            out of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+category"} distributive category
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            has finite coproducts then consider the category of finite product preserving functors
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sind
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi FP
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sind(\mathcal{C})\cong  FP(\mathcal{C}^{op},Set)
          %p
            The representable objects always preserves finite products, and so the Yoneda embedding
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}\to [\mathcal{C}^{op},Set]
            factors through
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sind
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sind(\mathcal{C})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sind
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sind(\mathcal{C})
            as the free sifted colimit completion of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
              is has sifted colimits then the Yoneda embedding induces a natural bijection between functors
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}\to \mathcal{D}
              and sifted-colimit-preserving functors
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Sind
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi 𝒟
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sind(\mathcal{C})\to \mathcal{D}
          %p The construction has another universal property, as the free colimit completion of a category respecting the existing finite coproducts.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
              is has colimits then the Yoneda embedding induces a natural bijection between finite-coproduct-preserving functors
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}\to \mathcal{D}
              and colimit-preserving-functors
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Sind
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi 𝒟
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sind(\mathcal{C})\to \mathcal{D}
          %h2#distributive_categories_and_cartesian_closure Distributive categories and cartesian closure
          %p
            The sifted-colimit-completion also gives a way of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful"} full and faithfully
            embedding a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+category"} distributive category
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed"} cartesian closed
            category, while preserving the structure. From a type-theoretic perspective, this shows that function types are a conservative extension of finite type theory.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              be a small category with finite coproducts and products. The following are equivalent.
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                  is a
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+category"} distributive category
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Sind
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sind(\mathcal{C})
                  is
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed"} cartesian closed
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+strongly+finitely+presentable+category"} locally strongly finitely presentable category
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+cocompletion"} free cocompletion
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ind-object"} ind-object
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li#AdamekRosickyVitale10
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jiri+Adamek"}> Jiri Adamek
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jiri+Rosicky"} Jiri Rosicky
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Enrico+Vitale"} Enrico Vitale
                = succeed "," do
                  %em What are sifted colimits?
                %strong 23
                (2010) pp. 251–260. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/23/13/23-13abs.html"}> web
                )
            %li#AdamekRosicky01
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jiri+Adamek"}> Jiri Adamek
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jiri+Rosicky"} Jiri Rosicky
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On sifted colimits and generalized varieties
                %strong 8
                (2001) pp 33–53. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/8/n3/8-03abs.html"}> web
                )
          %p The idea of product-preserving-functors as a cartesian closed category is in various sources, including:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marcelo+Fiore"}> Marcelo Fiore
                , “Enrichment and representation theorems for categories of domains and partial functions”. 1996.
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/~mpf23/papers/ADT/rep.ps.gz"} web
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Power"}> John Power
                , “Generic models for computational effects”, Theor. Comput. Sci. 364(2): 254-269 (2006)
            %li
              %p
                Younesse Kaddar, “Ideal distributors”, Section 1. 2020.
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://younesse.net/assets/ARPE_report.pdf"} report
            %li
              %p
                G. A. Kavvos,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Two-dimensional Kripke Semantics II: Stability and Completeness
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.03578"} arXiv:2406.03578
                (2024).
